@@ -26102,7 +26102,7 @@ msgstr ""
 #. Hades: help of setting "videoscreen.hades3doutput"; translated in Estuary (#31712)
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 msgctxt "#39978"
-msgid "For a 3D TV or projector. While Hades draws in a split 3D mode (over/under or side by side), the display is told so over HDMI and switches to 3D by itself. Frame packing, which keeps both eyes at full resolution, is used where the display and the graphics driver have it (Hades then draws over/under, also for a side-by-side video); otherwise the half format that is drawn. For a film in frame packing, turn on the adjustment of the display's refresh rate to the video, because HDMI has frame packing for 1080p at 24 Hz only. Where the display has no 3D mode at the refresh rate of a video (a 30p one, say), the 3D mode at twice that rate is used and each picture is shown twice. The display has to be on when Hades starts. Expect one more change of display mode when a 3D video starts and when it stops. Off: the two halves go out as an ordinary 2D picture and you switch the display to 3D by hand. Turn it off if a 3D video shows a black or broken picture. Without a 3D display nothing changes. Applies the next time the display mode is set."
+msgid "For a 3D TV or projector. While Hades draws in a split 3D mode (over/under or side by side), the display is told so over HDMI and switches to 3D by itself. Frame packing, which keeps both eyes at full resolution, is used where the display and the graphics driver have it (Hades then draws over/under, also for a side-by-side video); otherwise the half format that is drawn. For a film in frame packing, turn on 'Adjust display refresh rate', because HDMI has frame packing for 1080p at 24 Hz only. Where the display has no 3D mode at the refresh rate of a video (a 30p one, say), the 3D mode at twice that rate is used and each picture is shown twice. The display has to be on when Hades starts. Expect one more change of display mode when a 3D video starts and when it stops. Off: the two halves go out as an ordinary 2D picture and you switch the display to 3D by hand. Turn it off if a 3D video shows a black or broken picture. Without a 3D display nothing changes. Applies the next time the display mode is set."
 msgstr ""
 
 #. Hades: label of setting "videoscreen.hades3doutput"; translated in Estuary (#31711)
@@ -27254,7 +27254,7 @@ msgstr ""
 #. Hades: help of setting "videoscreen.hadeshlgasdv"
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 msgctxt "#41151"
-msgid "For a display that takes Dolby Vision. An HLG video can be sent as Dolby Vision instead of as HLG: Hades converts its picture to PQ as ITU-R BT.2100 says an HLG picture is shown on its reference display (1000 cd/m² and a system gamma of 1.2) and measures the peak, the average and the darkest level of each scene from that picture, as \"HDR10 as Dolby Vision, measured\" does. This changes the picture on purpose: the display maps it with its Dolby Vision processing instead of its HLG one, so brightness and contrast are not those of its HLG mode, and the metadata are not the studio's (an HLG video has none). Automatic (the default): only where this output cannot send HLG at all but can carry Dolby Vision, as some Intel graphics, so that the video keeps its HDR instead of being mapped to SDR; everywhere else HLG plays as HLG. On: wherever the display takes Dolby Vision. Off: HLG always plays as HLG. HDR10, HDR10+ and Dolby Vision videos are not affected. Applies from the next video."
+msgid "For a display that takes Dolby Vision. An HLG video can be sent as Dolby Vision instead of as HLG: Hades converts its picture to PQ as ITU-R BT.2100 says an HLG picture is shown on its reference display (1000 cd/m² and a system gamma of 1.2) and measures the peak, the average and the darkest level of each scene from that picture, as \"Make HDR10 videos Dolby Vision (measured)\" does. This changes the picture on purpose: the display maps it with its Dolby Vision processing instead of its HLG one, so brightness and contrast are not those of its HLG mode, and the metadata are not the studio's (an HLG video has none). Automatic (the default): only where this output cannot send HLG at all but can carry Dolby Vision, as some Intel graphics, so that the video keeps its HDR instead of being mapped to SDR; everywhere else HLG plays as HLG. On: wherever the display takes Dolby Vision. Off: HLG always plays as HLG. HDR10, HDR10+ and Dolby Vision videos are not affected. Applies from the next video."
 msgstr ""
 
